<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Women's Monument</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vrouemonument</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Women's Monument is a memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, unveiled on 16 December 1913. It commemorates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> women and children, some 26,000 by common estimate, who died in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> and bronze sculpture by Anton van Wouw became an important symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> remembrance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument.jpg" alt="National Women's Monument" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlviloria · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein_Women%27s_Memorial_Vil004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of the monument came from Martinus Steyn, former president of the Orange Free State, while he was being treated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the Boer War. His wife, Rachel Isabella Steyn, known as Tibbie, had relatives and friends who died in the camps and close ties with the British anti-war campaigner Emily Hobhouse. On his return Steyn set up a committee; a school or hospital was rejected as lacking inspiration, and £10,000 was raised from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> community between 1907 and 1911.</p><p>English-speaking members of the Bloemfontein Town Council delayed the start of building, feeling the memorial would reflect badly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and on the local people who had supported the war. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a>, who was pursuing reconciliation after the war, also disapproved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The monument, designed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> architect Frans Soff, stands about 3 km south of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>. It consists of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> about 35 m high and low semicircular walls on two sides. The bronze group shows a bareheaded woman holding her emaciated, dying child, while a second woman looks into the distance calling on the Lord. It was drawn from a scene Hobhouse witnessed at the Springfontein camp on 15 May 1901. Hobhouse thought little of Van Wouw as a sculptor and felt the child looked asleep rather than dying. About 20,000 people attended the unveiling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The burial of Steyn at its foot in 1916 began a new kind of commemoration. Christiaan de Wet was buried there in 1922, Hobhouse's ashes were placed there in 1926, and Tibbie Steyn, who had worried that soldiers' graves would dilute the focus on women and children, was herself buried beside her husband in 1955 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a>. Since 1960 other memorials of the war have joined it, and the Anglo-Boer War Museum stands on the same grounds. The monument was later overshadowed as a national symbol by the Voortrekker Monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, opened on 16 December 1949. It is a Provincial Heritage Site of the Free State.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Women's Monument” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bloemfontein became the judicial capital of South Africa in 1910, and the <b>National Women’s Monument</b> there, unveiled in 1913, commemorates the women and children who died in the camps of the Second Boer War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
